In article <1993May17.221944.15743@beaver.cs.washington.edu>, duyphan@cs.washington.edu (Haldan Orlav) writes:
> I like to make a few comments about named weapons.  It seems to me that 
> weapons that deal with specific types of creatures are pretty useless
> (e.g. Dragonbane, Giantslayer, Ogresmasher, etc.).  Unlike many other
> weapons which do extra damage against a vast majority of monsters, these
> are very limited in power.  I once tested my Dragonslayer against a black
> dragon, and it didn't seem to do any more damage than my Mjollnir, a little
> less, in fact, since my Mjollnir was better enchanted at the time.
> [text deleted]....
> 
Think this way:
If you have a choice between a long sword and a mace - which one do you take?
If you have a choice between Vorpal Blade and Ogresmasher - which one
  do you take (you already answered this one)
If you have a choice between a long sword and a Sunsword - which one do you
  take?

Your real problem is (IMHO) that the most powerful artifacts are often 
easier to obtain than the lesser ones - and often you find the lesser
ones already having the more powerful. In this case just do what
you normally do when finding a bec-de-corbin while carrying +2 katana
- just leave it alone (or - if paranoid - put it into some box).

A good question is: Should the game be adjusted such that the powerful
'general' artifacts are more difficult to obtain than those 'race specific'
ones? Probably...

